WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:01.343
So.

2
00:00:01.343 --> 00:00:03.270
You've got a few clankers running, and you're

3
00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.374
tokenmaxxing like everyone else.

4
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.659
To get anything done, they need to work

5
00:00:08.659 --> 00:00:11.180
in parallel, each on its own branch.

6
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.814
But in one checkout, they clash.

7
00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.600
One switches branches under another, and your login

8
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.760
fix lands on another agent's branch.

9
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.349
Duh!

10
00:00:22.349 --> 00:00:24.288
I'm not a caveman, you say.

11
00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:26.319
Git has worktrees for this.

12
00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:28.620
A fresh worktree, in about a second.

13
00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:31.346
But it starts empty.

14
00:00:31.346 --> 00:00:33.610
No dependencies, no dev shell, none of your

15
00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:34.818
local files.

16
00:00:34.818 --> 00:00:36.066
Just tumbleweeds.

17
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.660
So your agent improvises.

18
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.342
It reinstalls everything in every worktree.

19
00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:44.862
Or it gives up: it can't lint here,

20
00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:48.101
can't regenerate the protos here, so it reaches

21
00:00:48.101 --> 00:00:50.434
back into your main checkout and links things

22
00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:51.527
across.

23
00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:55.726
And the next agent rediscovers all of it,

24
00:00:55.726 --> 00:00:57.805
from scratch, every single time.

25
00:00:59.493 --> 00:01:01.478
On a big monorepo, that's over a minute

26
00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:04.741
and gigabytes of disk, per worktree.

27
00:01:04.741 --> 00:01:06.971
Your laptop fan has opinions.

28
00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.603
But there's a better way.

29
00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:12.917
Meet clove.

30
00:01:14.827 --> 00:01:17.416
clove keeps one golden image of your repo,

31
00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:21.062
with dependencies installed and the environment warmed.

32
00:01:21.062 --> 00:01:24.326
Each worktree is a copy-on-write clone of it.

33
00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:29.431
It detects your toolchain on its own: mise,

34
00:01:29.431 --> 00:01:33.611
direnv and nix, pnpm, bun, yarn, npm, uv

35
00:01:33.611 --> 00:01:36.234
and cargo, plus your local files.

36
00:01:36.234 --> 00:01:37.779
Anything else is a step in a small

37
00:01:37.779 --> 00:01:40.219
config file, shared with your team.

38
00:01:42.327 --> 00:01:44.812
And it stays warm on its own.

39
00:01:44.812 --> 00:01:47.656
Each time you pull, a background service refreshes

40
00:01:47.656 --> 00:01:50.758
the golden image, while your clones keep working.

41
00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.578
Under the hood, it leans on what your

42
00:01:54.578 --> 00:01:56.691
file system already does well.

43
00:01:56.691 --> 00:01:59.280
On macOS, each clone is an APFS disk

44
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.589
image, copied whole by a single clonefile call.

45
00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:05.782
On Linux, it's a btrfs snapshot, taken in

46
00:02:05.782 --> 00:02:07.340
a few milliseconds.

47
00:02:09.627 --> 00:02:11.252
So a new worktree is ready in about

48
00:02:11.252 --> 00:02:12.332
a second.

49
00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.526
Everything is already there, and your fan and

50
00:02:14.526 --> 00:02:17.140
your disk can finally relax.

51
00:02:19.627 --> 00:02:22.541
When a lockfile changes, only that step runs

52
00:02:22.541 --> 00:02:23.472
again.

53
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.890
Use it from the command line, from a

54
00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.315
full-screen terminal interface, or from the desktop app,

55
00:02:30.315 --> 00:02:32.059
on macOS and Linux.

56
00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.006
It brings your clones up to date, with

57
00:02:36.006 --> 00:02:38.618
your coding agent resolving the conflicts.

58
00:02:38.618 --> 00:02:41.497
It cleans up after merged pull requests.

59
00:02:41.497 --> 00:02:43.819
And it teaches your agents to use it,

60
00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:44.995
so you don't have to.

61
00:02:47.293 --> 00:02:48.268
That's clove.

62
00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:50.239
Your worktrees, ready in a second.

63
00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:52.153
Check it out at clove.fast.

